// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma
// VWK Trap Dispatcher — routes traps by vector number (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the x86_64 trap dispatcher. After trap_entry (K3a's
// trap_trampoline.vuma) saves the 22-word TrapFrame and calls into C
// with a pointer to it, control reaches trap_handler(tf) here. The
// dispatcher routes by `tf.vector` (the interrupt-vector number the
// trap_entry stub pushed — 0..255 on x86_64):
//
//     vector 0..31   → CPU exception  → trap_panic(tf)
//                                       (in real kernel: panic with msg;
//                                        K12 adds real panic; hosted: nop)
//     vector 32..127 → hardware IRQ    → trap_irq(tf)
//     vector 128     → syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64) → trap_syscall(tf)
//                                       (K3e provides the real syscall
//                                        table; hosted: nop)
//     vector 129..255→ hardware IRQ    → trap_irq(tf)
//
// The contract simplifies the routing: anything in [0,32) is an
// exception; 128 (int 0x80) is the syscall ABI; everything else in
// [32,256) \ {128} is treated as a hardware IRQ and handed to
// trap_irq, which calls irq_dispatch from the sibling irq.vuma module
// (re-declared locally — see "Why re-declare nothing here?" below).
//
// ── TrapFrame re-declaration (x86_64, 22 fields) ────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the x86_64
// TrapFrame byte-identically to womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap_trampoline.vuma
// (K3a). The layout is 22 u64 fields in the order trap_entry pushes them
// (15 GP regs rax..r15 + vector + error_code + rip + cs + rflags + rsp + ss
// = 22 × 8 = 176 bytes — the field order MUST match trap.S, which K11 will
// write to match this layout). See K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma header for
// the full push-order derivation (CPU-pushed + stub-pushed + trap_entry-
// pushed); the byte-identical-redeclaration invariant (K2c "extern
// signatures MUST stay byte-identical") extends to layouts — this file's
// `trap_handler(tf: State<TrapFrame>)` MUST see the same field offsets,
// field names, and field types as K3a's file.
//
// ── Why is this file separate from K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma? ──────────────
// Separation of concerns: K3a declares the TrapFrame LAYOUT + the extern
// "C" trampolines (idt_load, irq_mask, irq_unmask, pic_eoi, cr2_read) and
// the bare helpers (trap_frame_init, trap_page_fault_addr). K3d (this file)
// declares the DISPATCHER — the per-vector routing logic that decides
// whether a trap is a CPU exception, a syscall, or an IRQ. K3a is the
// arch-specific "what is a TrapFrame and how do I touch the PIC/CR2";
// K3d is the arch-agnostic "given a TrapFrame, what do I do with it". K3e
// (syscall dispatch) will add the syscall-number → handler-table lookup
// inside trap_syscall; K11 (bare-metal parity) will replace trap_panic's
// no-op with a real panic-on-exception path.
//
// ── Why is trap_irq a stub? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_irq is the IRQ-path entry point: in real kernel it would call
// irq_dispatch(tbl, vector - 32) from the sibling irq.vuma module. But
// VUMA has no `import` and the dispatcher doesn't have an IrqTable in
// hand (the IrqTable is a per-CPU global allocated by kmain and passed
// to irq_dispatch explicitly). In hosted mode, trap_irq is a no-op (the
// host kernel handles hardware IRQs — VUMA code never sees them). In
// bare-metal K11, trap.S will load the per-CPU IrqTable pointer into RDI
// before calling trap_handler, OR trap_handler will receive the IrqTable
// as a second State<T> parameter (K11 will pick one). For now, trap_irq
// is a no-op stub — this is NOT a violation of "no stubs" because the
// DoD explicitly says "IRQ handler stub — calls irq_dispatch (re-declared
// locally). For now, just return (stub)." K11 removes the stub.
//
// ── Why is trap_syscall a stub? ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_syscall is the syscall-path entry point: in real kernel it reads
// tf.rax (syscall number), indexes into a syscall table, calls the handler
// with tf.rdi (arg0), tf.rsi (arg1), tf.rdx (arg2), and writes the return
// value back to tf.rax. K3e will provide the syscall table + dispatch
// logic. For now, trap_syscall is a no-op stub (the DoD says "Syscall
// handler stub — K3e provides the real implementation"). Same "not a
// violation" rationale as trap_irq.
//
// ── Why is trap_panic a stub? ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// trap_panic is the CPU-exception path: in real kernel it would print a
// panic message with the trap info (vector, error_code, rip) and halt.
// K12 (the panic/log wave) adds real panic; until then, trap_panic is a
// no-op stub (the DoD says "CPU exception handler — just return (in real
// kernel: panic with msg)"). Hosted mode never delivers real CPU
// exceptions (the host kernel does), so the stub is never exercised from
// the self-test except as a "did we crash?" smoke check.
//
// ── Why nested ifs instead of else-if? ──────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a) supports `if cond { ... } else { ... }`
// but not `else if` chained. The dispatcher therefore uses nested ifs:
//     if v < 32 { panic } else { if v == 128 { syscall } else { irq } }
// This is the same nested-if pattern K3c's trap_is_page_fault uses for
// its range check (12 <= scause <= 15 → nested-if form).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// TrapFrame is a 22-word struct held in a `___pmt_buffer`-backed
// State<TrapFrame>; the CALLER allocates it via `state_new(TrapFrame)`
// and passes it by reference to `trap_handler` / `trap_frame_init`
// (init-style API — see "Why init-style?" in womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma for
// the codegen rationale). No externs are declared in this file (the
// dispatcher doesn't directly touch the PIC or CR2 — those are K3a's
// trap_trampoline.vuma's externs, used by trap_page_fault_addr in K3a
// and by the K3e/K11 page-fault handler). The dispatcher's sub-handlers
// (trap_panic, trap_syscall, trap_irq) take State<TrapFrame> by regular
// VUMA-fn borrow (NOT an extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need the
// attribute, only extern "C" calls do, because the codegen tracks the
// State's lifetime through regular fn calls but conservatively
// invalidates on extern calls). None of the sub-handlers call any extern,
// so there is no use-after-invalidate risk.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares TrapFrame byte-identically
// to K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma and re-declares trap_frame_init
// byte-identically (the dispatcher's self-test needs a zeroed TrapFrame
// to test vector routing, and the init helper is the canonical way to
// zero one — same pattern as K3a's self-test). The sibling irq.vuma
// module (also K3d) re-declares the irq_mask/irq_unmask/pic_eoi externs
// byte-identically to K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma; this file does NOT
// re-declare those externs because the dispatcher's hosted-mode sub-
// handlers (trap_panic, trap_syscall, trap_irq) are all no-ops and don't
// call any extern. K11 will swap the stubs for real handlers that DO
// call the externs; at that point K11 will either re-declare the externs
// in this file or pass them through the call chain from kmain.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL (32, 128) per the K3d
// contract's "IMPORTANT" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14 and
// rip=4096). The constants:
//     32  = boundary between CPU exceptions (0..31) and IRQs (32..255)
//           on x86_64 (the 8259 PIC's IRQs are remapped to vectors 32..47
//           by the IDT setup in K11's boot path)
//     128 = 0x80 = the int 0x80 syscall vector on x86_64 (Linux's legacy
//           32-bit syscall ABI; modern Linux uses SYSCALL/SYSRET but the
//           VUMA kernel follows the simpler int 0x80 convention for
//           portability across all three arches — aarch64 uses SVC #0,
//           riscv64 uses ECALL from U-mode; the dispatcher's per-arch
//           re-declaration will check vector==128 on x86_64,
//           trap_is_syscall (EC==0x15) on aarch64, and scause==8 on
//           riscv64)
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/trap/trap.vuma \
//         /tmp/trap.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/trap.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the 3 self-test dispatches all succeed
// without crashing — vector 0 → trap_panic, vector 128 → trap_syscall,
// vector 32 → trap_irq).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The x86_64 TrapFrame. Re-declared byte-identically to K3a's
// trap_trampoline.vuma. The field order MUST match trap.S exactly (K11
// will write trap.S to match). 22 fields × 8 bytes = 176 bytes. See the
// header comment for the push-order derivation (CPU-pushed + stub-pushed
// + trap_entry-pushed).
layout TrapFrame = {
    rax: u64,
    rbx: u64,
    rcx: u64,
    rdx: u64,
    rsi: u64,
    rdi: u64,
    rbp: u64,
    r8: u64,
    r9: u64,
    r10: u64,
    r11: u64,
    r12: u64,
    r13: u64,
    r14: u64,
    r15: u64,
    vector: u64,      // interrupt vector number (0..255)
    error_code: u64,  // CPU error code (0 if the exception pushes none)
    rip: u64,         // instruction pointer at the trap
    cs: u64,          // code segment
    rflags: u64,      // flags register
    rsp: u64,         // stack pointer
    ss: u64,          // stack segment
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Helpers.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Init-style API (caller allocates `tf` via state_new(TrapFrame), passes
// by reference). Re-declared byte-identically to K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma
// because this file's self-test needs a zeroed TrapFrame and the helper
// is the canonical way to zero one (the contract says "Re-declare
// trap_frame_init (from K3a)" — same pattern as the no-import rule above).
// Writes all 22 fields to 0.

// Initialize a TrapFrame to zeros. Useful both for the self-test and
// for the early-boot allocator (the trap dispatcher can call this on a
// scratch TrapFrame before populating it from the live save area, so
// stale stack garbage in the unused-error-code case can't leak into the
// handler).
fn trap_frame_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    tf.rax = 0;
    tf.rbx = 0;
    tf.rcx = 0;
    tf.rdx = 0;
    tf.rsi = 0;
    tf.rdi = 0;
    tf.rbp = 0;
    tf.r8 = 0;
    tf.r9 = 0;
    tf.r10 = 0;
    tf.r11 = 0;
    tf.r12 = 0;
    tf.r13 = 0;
    tf.r14 = 0;
    tf.r15 = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.rip = 0;
    tf.cs = 0;
    tf.rflags = 0;
    tf.rsp = 0;
    tf.ss = 0;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Trap dispatcher.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Routes by `tf.vector`. The three branches are documented in the file
// header. The nested-if structure (instead of else-if) is required by
// VUMA's if-expression syntax (K2a — see "Why nested ifs" above).
// `tf` is passed by regular VUMA-fn borrow to all sub-handlers; none of
// the sub-handlers call any extern, so the State's lifetime is preserved
// across the calls (the codegen's conservative invalidation only fires
// on extern "C" calls).

// Trap dispatcher: routes by vector number.
//   vector 0..31   → CPU exception → trap_panic
//   vector 32..255 → IRQ or syscall → trap_irq / trap_syscall
fn trap_handler(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    let v = tf.vector;
    if v < 32 {
        // CPU exception — panic path.
        trap_panic(tf);
    } else {
        if v == 128 {
            // Syscall (int 0x80 on x86_64).
            trap_syscall(tf);
        } else {
            // Hardware IRQ.
            trap_irq(tf);
        }
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-vector-class sub-handlers (hosted-mode stubs — K3e/K11/K12 fill in).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// CPU exception handler — just return (in real kernel: panic with msg).
// K12 adds the real panic path (print the trap frame + halt). Hosted
// mode never delivers real CPU exceptions, so this stub is never
// exercised from the self-test except as a "did we crash?" check.
fn trap_panic(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // In hosted mode, just return. K12 adds real panic.
    return;
}

// Syscall handler stub — K3e provides the real implementation.
// K3e will read tf.rax (syscall number), index into a syscall table,
// call the handler with tf.rdi/rsi/rdx (args 0..2), and write the
// return value back to tf.rax. Hosted mode never delivers syscalls via
// int 0x80 (the host kernel uses its own syscall ABI), so this stub is
// never exercised from the self-test except as a "did we crash?" check.
fn trap_syscall(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // In hosted mode, just return.
    return;
}

// IRQ handler stub — calls irq_dispatch (re-declared locally).
// In real kernel (K11), trap_irq would:
//   1. Read tf.vector
//   2. Subtract 32 (the PIC's vector-base offset) to get the IRQ number
//   3. Look up the per-CPU IrqTable
//   4. Call irq_dispatch(tbl, irq) (from the sibling irq.vuma module)
// For now, just return (stub). K11 removes the stub.
fn trap_irq(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    // K3d irq.vuma provides irq_dispatch.
    // For now, just return (stub).
    return;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a TrapFrame via state_new, zero it via trap_frame_init, then
// exercise all three dispatcher branches:
//   (1) vector 0   → trap_panic path (CPU exception)
//   (2) vector 128 → trap_syscall path (int 0x80 syscall)
//   (3) vector 32  → trap_irq path (timer IRQ, the first PIC vector)
// Each dispatch is a "did we crash?" smoke check — the hosted-mode sub-
// handlers are all no-ops, so the only failure mode is a codegen/IVE
// issue (e.g. invalid State<TrapFrame> access, malformed if-expression
// lowering). If all three dispatches complete without crashing, main
// returns 0.
//
// Per the K3d contract's "IMPORTANT" rule: all non-zero constants in the
// self-test use DECIMAL (32, 128) — decimal avoids the parser's hex-
// literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14 and
// rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    trap_frame_init(tf);

    // Test 1: vector 0 (CPU exception) → trap_panic (no crash).
    tf.vector = 0;
    trap_handler(tf);

    // Test 2: vector 128 (syscall) → trap_syscall (no crash).
    tf.vector = 128;
    trap_handler(tf);

    // Test 3: vector 32 (IRQ) → trap_irq (no crash).
    tf.vector = 32;
    trap_handler(tf);

    return 0;
}
